\subtree{
\title{6. Measurements (host JVM, C2/Graal as configured by the build; not the arena container)}

\p{Elaboration of everything (\code{\verb~|M7CorpusTest~}; rent-to-own would elaborate about 15\% of it):}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{constants}\th{blocks (incl. sub-blocks)}\th{code}\th{TELE}\th{BODY}\th{RULE}\th{RULES}}
\tr{\td{init-prelude}\td{2,121}\td{7,698}\td{1.3 MiB}\td{1,975}\td{1,441}\td{125}\td{104}}
\tr{\td{Init}\td{59,979}\td{124,411}\td{41.8 MiB}\td{56,154 (8.56 M words, 152 words each)}\td{17,284 (56 words each)}\td{603}\td{1,400}}
\tr{\td{Std}\td{103,885}\td{215,164}\td{88.1 MiB}\td{97,062}\td{30,068}\td{1,046}\td{2,209}}
}

\p{Elaboration time was not measured in a way that supports a number: \code{\verb~|M7CorpusTest~} reports wall clock per constant tried, and three runs of the same code on this host gave 1.6, 5.3 and 12.9 us per TELE on Init (0.6 to 1.3 us per BODY); Std, which runs first in the JVM and pays the JIT warm-up, 14.7 to 41.8 us. The ISA's spike measured 3.0 us per telescope; these runs neither confirm nor contradict it.}

\p{CPU time of the machine on Init with and without K1 (\code{\verb~|M7BenchTest.init~}: one session, one thread, 58,547 units, the CPU time of that thread from the JVM, a fresh engine per pass so elaboration is inside the time, v0 sessions (reset per declaration), warm-up pass discarded; host JVM, load average 70 to 160 while measuring, so a difference below about 10\% is not resolved; every configuration accepted all 58,547 units). The generic tier changed twice while K1 was being measured, and the answer changed with it:}

\table{
\tr{\th{generic tier of}\th{switches}\th{ELAB_AT}\th{CPU}\th{ratio}\th{blocks}\th{activations}\th{ticks}}
\tr{\td{09:55Z (PI record + ENPI + DOM + INST per argument)}\td{none (v0)}\td{-}\td{11.9 s}\td{1.00}\td{0}\td{0}\td{93.6 M}}
\tr{\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|tele~}}\td{10}\td{10.8 s}\td{0.91}\td{11,721}\td{5.0 M}\td{79.4 M}}
\tr{\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|code,casetrees~}}\td{10}\td{10.0 s}\td{0.84}\td{6,425}\td{10.5 M}\td{82.3 M}}
\tr{\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|code,tele,casetrees~}}\td{10}\td{8.3 s}\td{0.69}\td{17,997}\td{15.9 M}\td{68.3 M}}
\tr{\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|code,tele,casetrees~}}\td{1}\td{8.3 s}\td{0.69}\td{79,733}\td{16.2 M}\td{67.2 M}}
\tr{\td{10:55Z (M5's telescope-mode TWALK and grouped VTYPE, M2's let fix), best of 5}\td{none (v0)}\td{-}\td{8.35 s}\td{1.00}\td{0}\td{0}\td{77.8 M}}
\tr{\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|tele~}}\td{10}\td{9.18 s}\td{1.10}\td{11,652}\td{5.0 M}\td{78.0 M}}
\tr{\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|code,casetrees~}}\td{10}\td{8.39 s}\td{1.01}\td{6,425}\td{10.4 M}\td{66.5 M}}
\tr{\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|code,tele,casetrees~}}\td{10}\td{7.98 s}\td{0.96}\td{17,930}\td{15.7 M}\td{67.0 M}}
\tr{\td{}\td{\code{\verb~|code,tele,casetrees~}}\td{1}\td{7.56 s}\td{0.91}\td{79,765}\td{16.1 M}\td{66.6 M}}
}

\p{Reading. Against the generic tier of the morning K1 saved a third of the CPU time on Init. M5 then gave the generic TWALK what the TELE role is made of (it walks the syntactic telescope, no PI record, no ENPI / INST per argument), which took the generic tier from 11.9 s to 8.35 s; against that tier the K1 roles together measured 4 to 9\% less CPU on Init and the body / rule / case-tree roles alone no difference (1.01x, ticks -14\%). \code{\verb~|tele~} alone measured 1.10x in that run, which is at the edge of what this host resolves and was not reproduced elsewhere (1.00x on the Cedar slice below, 0.92x to 0.99x on init-prelude, ticks equal): what can be said is that \strong{TELE shows no CPU gain over M5's telescope-mode TWALK}, not that it costs 10\%. Why it does not gain is not established. One conjecture, not tested: the generic walk's tabled EVAL of a domain answers from its memo where the block builds the domain again. The profile below rules out the stepper's own overhead as the main term. This is not what the ISA's telescope spike predicted (-7\% to -28\% of an instantiation pass). Init is the regression corpus, not the yardstick (owner note 9). The Cedar slice (\code{\verb~|cedar-hot-ax.ndjson~}, 8,418 units, the same harness, best of 2, generic tier of 10:55Z; every configuration accepted all units):}

\table{
\tr{\th{switches}\th{ELAB_AT}\th{CPU}\th{ratio}\th{session memory made}\th{blocks}\th{activations}}
\tr{\td{none (v0)}\td{-}\td{3.02 s}\td{1.00}\td{817 MiB (1.00)}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|tele~}}\td{10}\td{3.03 s}\td{1.00}\td{0.97}\td{5,148}\td{1.33 M}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|code,casetrees~}}\td{10}\td{2.99 s}\td{0.99}\td{0.92}\td{5,091}\td{1.15 M}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|code,tele,casetrees~}}\td{10}\td{2.93 s}\td{0.97}\td{0.88}\td{10,183}\td{2.65 M}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|code,tele,casetrees~}}\td{1}\td{2.90 s}\td{0.96}\td{0.87}\td{27,367}\td{2.81 M}}
}

\p{K1 makes 12 to 13\% less session memory (records, runs; a count, not disturbed by the load). The CPU differences of 1 to 4\% are inside this host's noise: no CPU gain is established on the Cedar slice. On init-prelude a TELE's MATCH answers T 2,371 times and "unknown" 239 times; the argument's type is the built domain itself 22,318 times and CONV is called 3,328 times (\code{\verb~|Code.Ext.matchT / matchF / targSame / targConv~}).}

\p{Closed evaluation is where K1 pays: \code{\verb~|reachability.ndjson~} (4,413 units, the same harness, one measured pass after the warm-up, generic tier of 11:10Z; every configuration accepted all units):}

\table{
\tr{\th{switches}\th{CPU}\th{ratio}\th{session memory made}\th{ticks}\th{blocks}\th{activations}}
\tr{\td{none (v0)}\td{6.68 s}\td{1.00}\td{1,369 MiB (1.00)}\td{34.6 M}\td{0}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|code~}}\td{3.65 s}\td{0.55}\td{0.60}\td{29.1 M}\td{1,721}\td{7.97 M}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|code,casetrees~}}\td{2.26 s}\td{\strong{0.34}}\td{0.38}\td{16.5 M}\td{1,770}\td{4.43 M}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|code,tele,casetrees~}}\td{2.25 s}\td{0.34}\td{0.37}\td{16.5 M}\td{3,290}\td{4.63 M}}
}

\p{Bodies and rules as blocks halve the CPU time, the case trees (B8: the motive, the unchosen alternatives and the \code{\verb~|casesOn~} / \code{\verb~|rec~} applications of a matcher are never built) take it to a third. This is the ISA's prediction (0.1 "(b) the reduction roles carry the tail", "(c) derived rules matter on closed evaluation only"; nano-lean's rules were worth 1.9x there).}

\p{Where the time goes with \code{\verb~|tele~} on Init (\code{\verb~|M7ProfTest~}: JFR samples of the checking thread, 2,056 samples; \code{\verb~|work/status/machine-M7-prof-init-tele.txt~}): self time \code{\verb~|Runs.pruneNear~} 18\%, \code{\verb~|Values.closure~} 17\%, \code{\verb~|Values.neu~} 13\%, \code{\verb~|Pairs.status~} 6\%, \code{\verb~|Machine.reset~} 5\%; \code{\verb~|Code.resumeAt~} 1.2\%, all of \code{\verb~|Code~} and \code{\verb~|Match~} together about 2\%. So about half of the samples are in interning, and the stepper's own code is a small share. \code{\verb~|Eval.table~} (the tabling probe of the generic EVAL) is 21.5\% inclusive. The callers of \code{\verb~|pruneNear~} / \code{\verb~|closure~} / \code{\verb~|neu~} were not broken down, so how much of the interning belongs to the tabling of proof terms (which no code role of a constant touches) and how much to values the blocks themselves build is an inference, not a measurement.}

\p{Not measured: the arena container, Std, warm sessions (step 1), gated tabling (which removes most of that tabling and will change these ratios again).}

\p{Verdicts with K1 (\code{\verb~|code,tele,casetrees~}, ELAB_AT 1 and 10, M8's differential runner): tutorial 141 files and bugs 34 files / 1,147 checks DIFF-CLEAN, also under D = 0 (\code{\verb~|M7HookedTest~}); all 230 published tests on the main tree of 11:10Z (\code{\verb~|report.sh -Dm8.switches=tele,code,casetrees -Drlvm.machine.elabAt=1 all~}, \code{\verb~|work/status/machine-diff-all-M7k1.txt~}): no wrong verdict, 229 files clean, 1 declined (\code{\verb~|good/perf/loop-unfolded-once~}, E_MEM: 2^28 run words; v0's report of 10:47Z declines the same file the same way), 17,492 of 17,493 units agree, the machine rejects 41 declarations and the reference rejects the same ones; Init, the Cedar slice and reachability: every unit accepted by every configuration of the tables above; M8's convention report with K1 on tutorial: EQUIVALENT across D = 64 / 0 / unbounded; M8's tiers report on tutorial: EQUIVALENT (175 constants, 700 results).}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Not done, shortcuts, open points}

\p{(Wave 4 list; section 8.6 is the current one.)}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|Cargs~} does not read comparison plans yet (M4): \code{\verb~|Cong~} is tested on fact records only.}
\li{\code{\verb~|FIELDS(S)~} (ISA 3.2, for IPROJ) is not elaborated; IPROJ stays generic.}
\li{\code{\verb~|B9~} inside TELE (CHECK of a lambda argument against the domain) is not implemented: TARG always infers and converts (the admissible fallback).}
\li{\code{\verb~|TARG.EXPECT~} (the result-pattern shortcut) is emitted and implemented, but no caller passes an expected type yet (the hook passes NONE), so it has only been exercised as a no-op.}
\li{Terms of the generated region cannot be operands (28-bit payload): a role that mentions one stays generic.}
\li{Tier equivalence of value ids is "up to PEEK" in principle (the generic walk itself is path-dependent when a suspension's value becomes known between two reads); on the corpora above every compared id was identical. A static-beta binder aliases its argument's operand instead of copying the PEEKed value.}
\li{TELE blocks are large (152 words on Init): the partial-application entries are a third of that.}
\li{The IC table (\code{\verb~|m.ic~}) is used by KVAL and, since wave 6, by KCONST of a level-polymorphic constant; a site beyond pc 4095 bypasses it.}
\li{Heat counters per block (ISA 8.3): since wave 6 \code{\verb~|Tier.heat(engine)~} (section 8.2); off unless asked for.}
\li{\code{\verb~|dumpStats~} of the engine does not print the code store's numbers (M6's file).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Wave 6 (2026-11-24, 13:25Z - 16:55Z): what code objects are for}

\p{Everything in this section was measured on the CE native image (\code{\verb~|-O3~}, built by \code{\verb~|work/m7/mkdist.sh~}: the pipeline jars of the integrator's \code{\verb~|work/machine-int/dist-g-native~}, the machine classes of the M7 sandbox) through the launcher, in the arena-shaped container (\code{\verb~|work/m7/m.py~}, a copy of the integrator's tool writing \code{\verb~|work/m7/measure.tsv~}; batches \code{\verb~|work/m7/batch{0,2,3}.sh~}, each under one hold of the measurement lock). The host ran 15 to 30 other agents' builds and runs the whole time (load average 150 to 230 from 14:00Z on), so CPU seconds are comparable inside one batch only; instructions per run (\code{\verb~|m.py --free~}: the same container without the lock and without the pinned cores) are the stable signal and are given beside them. One worker unless said otherwise.}

