}
}

\subtree{
\title{S3-F7: a capture list with an immediate operand (INT7, 2026-11-25)}

\p{\code{\verb~|Code.raw~} (the operand of a capture list, called from \code{\verb~|Code.mkClosure~}) had no case for \code{\verb~|Op.MODE_I~}. \code{\verb~|Elab.atom~} emits an immediate operand for a numeral below 2^28, and \code{\verb~|Elab.let~} / an inlined beta step bind a variable to the ARGV of its value, so a closure in a compiled body that captures a variable bound to a small numeral (\code{\verb~|let x := 5; m _ n (fun _ => x) ..~}) reached the default branch: \code{\verb~|E_INTERNAL~}, the unit was declined and the reference engine decided it (finding S3-F7 of reviewer S3; no wrong verdict). The case now returns the immediate value \code{\verb~|n | Layout.IMM~} - what \code{\verb~|Code.val~} returns for the operand and what the generic walk pushes for the binding (\code{\verb~|Eval.letFrom~} -> \code{\verb~|argvFast~} -> \code{\verb~|Lits.klit~}), so the frame is the run \code{\verb~|PRUNE~} builds (decision 6). Regression test \code{\verb~|M7CaptureLiteralTest~} (S3's eight minimal rows and the four random programs that found it, under \code{\verb~|code~}, \code{\verb~|code,casetrees~}, \code{\verb~|default~}, \code{\verb~|default,eager~}: no decline).}
}
